  "stimulus": "<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Recordings of Female Bottlenose Dolphins with Their Calves</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Dolphin ID</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Recording year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB07</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2012</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB25</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1989</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB43</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1992</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB79</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2018</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>In a study of bottlenose dolphins, biologist Laela <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"S\">S.</span> Sayigh and a team of researchers analyzed recordings of female bottlenose dolphins interacting with their calves.</p>",
